==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[God-speed]]
* [[God speed]]
* [[godspeed]]
* God's speed

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} phrase {{term|God spede||may God cause you to succeed|lang=enm}}, from {{term|God||god|lang=enm}} + {{term|spede|lang=enm}}, subjunctive of {{term|speden||to prosper|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|spēdan}}, from {{term|sped|spēd|success}} (see {{term|speed}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɡɒdˈspiːd/|lang=en}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{non-gloss definition|An expression of goodwill when addressing someone, typically someone about to start a journey or a daring endeavor.}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=Evangelist, after he had kissed him, gave him one smile, and bid him '''God-speed'''.|author=John Bunyan|title=The Pilgrim's Progress|year=1678}}
#* {{quote-book|passage="I'm wishing you '''God-speed''', Hattersley," cried Arthur, "and aiding you with my prayers."|author=Anne Brontë|title=The Tenant of Wildfell Hall|year=1848}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=Rowland at the garden gate was giving his hostess '''Godspeed''' on her way to church.|author=Henry James|title=Roderick Hudson|year=1879}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=Eight years before he had seen his friend off at the North Wall and wished him '''God-speed'''.|author=James Joyce|title=Dubliners|year=1914}}
#* {{quote-book|passage='''Godspeed''', John Glenn.|author=Scott Carpenter|date=February 20, 1962}}

